{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And these are the generations of Isaac, son of Abraham.</b> Even though it says that he was the son of Abraham, nonetheless it was necessary to say, 'Abraham begot Isaac' — because concerning Ishmael it is written, 'בֶּן אַבְרָהָם אֲשֶׁר יָלְדָה הָגָר הַמִּצְרִית' (Genesis 25:12). See that it used the expression 'son' in connection with Abraham, but the begetting it attributed to Hagar. Here it was necessary to say that both the form of 'son' and the 'begetting' — everything is traced after Abraham. For in the Hebrew language there is a distinction between the expression 'son' and the expression 'begetting': the expression 'son' is sometimes used even without begetting, for disciples are called sons even though he did not literally give birth to them, as in 'וַיְהִי לָהּ לְבֵן' of Moses, 'וַתְּהִי לוֹ לְבַת' of Esther, and Abraham is called 'father of a multitude of nations' even though he did not give birth to them. And likewise there is a difference between one who teaches and one who literally begets: for what a person receives of his nature from his teacher is incidental and not essential, and the incidental changes according to the receiver. But what a person receives of his nature from the one who begot him is essential and will not change if he is left thus upon his nature — unless through great diligence and effort and habituation he may arrive at a second nature. But the nature from his teacher changes easily. Therefore Moses said (Numbers 11:12), 'הֶאָנֹכִי הָרִיתִי אֵת כָּל הָעָם הַזֶּה, אִם אָנֹכִי יְלִדְתִּיהוּ' — and he said: even though they are my disciples and disciples are called sons, nonetheless I did not literally give birth to them, so what shall I do if their deeds change from good to evil, for they devised counsel and not from me, and the matter did not go forth from me? And if so, why do You cast all this burden upon me as though their guilt hangs on me? And what is written (Numbers 3:1), 'וְאֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדוֹת אַהֲרֹן וּמֹשֶׁה' and it counts Nadab and Abihu — it was necessary to use the expression 'generations' on account of Aaron, who begot them." "Therefore Ishmael was called only 'son of Abraham,' for he did not receive his nature except from what Abraham taught him through his good deeds, and this was in him incidental and changed — for in the end he turned to an evil way of life. Therefore it attributed the begetting to Hagar, for he received the nature of Hagar the Egyptian, and the Egyptians are steeped in licentiousness; therefore he too was jesting in sexual immorality and did not change, for he received her nature in essence. Therefore it attributed the incidental to Abraham and the essence to Hagar. But Isaac received the nature of Abraham and also learned from his deeds; therefore it attributed everything to Abraham — both the expression 'son' and the expression 'begetting.' And therefore he was not like Ishmael, who was jesting in sexual immorality even when he was a youth, for thus Scripture called him at that time (Genesis 21:12); and Scripture tells us concerning Isaac that he received his father's nature completely and was so fenced off from immorality that he subdued his urge and did not marry a woman until he was forty years old — for he waited until he found Rebecca his destined match, and did not wish to intermarry with the Canaanites, who inherited licentiousness from Ham their father who lay with Noah his father. Therefore he had to wait forty years, for without doubt for an only child such as him there were many who came forward, and how did he sit without a wife for such a long time? Rather, surely for the reason we have mentioned." "And lest the doubter find an opening to object and say: if Isaac was born in such holiness and purity, then how did Esau emerge from him, who hunted married women — the answer is that certainly Sarah was impregnated by Abimelech, therefore one son followed after the nature of Sarah, and the second after the nature of Abimelech. For on this account our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 87a) that he was a flattener of faces who resembled Abraham. Now according to our approach, in order to remove this speculation, Scripture said, 'בְּקַחְתּוֹ אֶת רִבְקָה בַּת בְּתוּאֵל הָאֲרַמִּי מִפַּדַּן אֲרָם אֲחוֹת לָבָן הָאֲרַמִּי' (Genesis 25:20). And most children follow after the mother's brothers; therefore one of the sons received the nature of Laban the deceiver, the trickster. And according to the straightforward reading it appears that for this reason it mentioned all three of them, to resolve what we find as three negative traits that were in Esau and where they came from: for the fact that he hunted married women, he inherited from Bethuel his mother's father, who violated all the virgins first, as is found in the Yalkut (Shime'oni, Chayei Sarah 109), and likewise in Chizkuni this midrash is cited. And that he said, 'הַלְעִיטֵנִי נָא' etc. and he was among gluttons, drunkards, and revellers — this trait he inherited by virtue of his mother's family being from Paddan-aram, as deduced in Genesis Rabbah in the portion of Lech Lecha (39:8): 'At the time Abraham sojourned in Aram Naharaim and in Aram Nahor, he saw them eating and drinking and revelling; he said, let not my lot be in this land.' And the fact that Esau was a deceiver who deceived his father, he inherited from Laban the Aramean, who was a scoundrel and a deceiver, as is well known." "<b>Abraham begot Isaac.</b> 'Begot' is a transitive (hif'il) form, even though grammatically the male 'gives seed to' the female; nonetheless we have already found the root ילד used also for males, as in 'וּכְנַעַן יָלַד' (Genesis 10:15), and likewise many in the portion of Noah — so why did it write here הוֹלִיד? Rather, it wanted to say that Abraham caused Isaac to have children and seed, for it is written 'וַיֶּעְתַּר יִצְחָק לַה׳' etc. (Genesis 25:21) and it is written 'וַיֵּעָתֶר לוֹ ה׳' — meaning, to him and not to her, for the prayer of a righteous man, son of a righteous man, etc. And accordingly, by virtue of Isaac being the son of the righteous man Abraham, therefore the merit of Abraham stood for him, that HaShem heard his prayer. And accordingly it is well said, 'Abraham begot Isaac' — Abraham caused that Isaac begot seed. But it has not yet explained how Abraham was the cause; therefore it explained the matter by saying, 'וַיֶּעְתַּר יִצְחָק לַה׳ לְנֹכַח אִשְׁתּוֹ' etc. — for from this he took proof for the statement 'Abraham begot Isaac.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:23.041493600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}